{
  "about": "The gloss dictionary. Each code has a full word ('words', the default style) and a short word ('short', the compact style). Full words are lower case and spelled out: a group, then the action, with a third part for less common codes. There is a fixed set of groups: run (moves and other things done on that line: run.rapid, run.feed, run.pause, run.home), set (modes that stay in effect: set.inch, set.abs, set.fpr, set.sfm, set.mirror.on), cycle (canned cycles and patterns: cycle.drill, cycle.peck, cycle.live.tap), call, flow, prog (program structure, messages and graphics), spindle, cool, tool, tlen, ccomp, work, axis, machine (builder extras such as chucks, tailstock, barriers, doors and outputs: machine.chuck.clamp, machine.tailstock.in), probe, thread, torch, param, pattern and scale. Options are the same words everywhere: on, off, fwd, rev, left, right, clamp, unclamp, in, out, select. The same meaning has the same full word on every control, so a code that means something else on one control gets a different word there. Short words come from the full word by one table: each group has a fixed core (S spindle, C coolant, T tool, TL tool length, TR cutter comp, WRK work offset, AX axis, M machine, PRB probe, TH thread, PL torch, Q parameter, PAT pattern; none for run, set, cycle, call, flow and prog), then an optional alternate (S2 second spindle, SL live tool, SG gear) and the option, each part 1 to 4 characters and the whole word 3 to 8 (SFWD, S2FWD, CON, TLON, TRL, WRK1, INCH, INCR, MTSIN). Two letters are used only for a standard abbreviation or a two-letter word: MM, XY, XZ, YZ, IF, DO. A code whose full word is not used by any code in the base (Fanuc-style) tables is control-specific, and the gloss row marks it. A word with a value is its name, a colon and the value in full words (tool:1, movx:-0.4215, incz:0.5, speed:2100, feed:16.0), with no unit after it; short words run the name and value together and show the unit (T1, iZ0.5, S2100 rpm). N numbers read as written (N10). A code whose meaning differs between machine types has 'mill' and 'lathe' entries. When codes share a gloss word, typing it gives the one marked 'preferred' (M30 for prog.end). Machine definition files can override any entry; an older file with a 'long' word still reads, using it as the full word. Each control's own words are in its file in data/controls/ ('words', keyed by the control id 'haas' or the id and machine type 'okuma.mill'; the more specific key wins), which scripts/make-ui-data.mjs joins into machineOverrides.",
